crates/op-plugins/src/state_pl…252144 tokens truncated…\n\nWindows:\n\ntext\n%APPDATA%\\gcloud\nmacOS / Linux:\n\ntext\n~/.config/gcloud\nTroubleshooting Tip\nIf you are asking this because you are stuck in an authentication loop or facing login issues, deleting the specific \"file\" might not be enough due to the OS keychain integration.\n\nThe cleanest way to reset your authentication state is usually:\n\nSign out via the Cloud Code status bar in VS Code.\nRun the \"Cloud Code: Sign Out\" command from the Command Palette (Ctrl+Shift+P / Cmd+Shift+P).\nIf issues persist, you may need to manually remove the googlecloudtools.cloudcode entry from your OS Keychain/Credential Manager.\nGenerated by Gemini 3 Pro Preview\n\njeremy hobson\nwhat scopes does vscode code assist extension use\n\n\nGemini\nResponding with gemini-3-pro\nThinking\nThe Gemini Code Assist features in VS Code are delivered through the Google Cloud Code extension. To function correctly—allowing you to not only chat with the AI but also deploy applications, view logs, and manage resources—the extension requests a specific set of OAuth 2.0 scopes during the login process.\n\nHere are the primary scopes used:\n\n1. The \"Cloud Platform\" Scope\nScope URL: https://www.googleapis.com/auth/cloud-platform\n\nThis is the most critical scope. It grants the extension full access to view and manage your data across Google Cloud Platform services.\n\nWhy it is needed: Gemini Code Assist needs this to interact with the Gemini for Google Cloud API (specifically the cloudaicompanion.googleapis.com service).\nBroader Context: Since the extension is also a general-purpose DevOps tool, this scope allows it to deploy to Cloud Run, manage GKE clusters, read Cloud Storage buckets, and view Cloud Logging entries without needing you to re-authenticate for every single service.\n2. User Identity Scopes\nScope URLs:\n\nemail\nprofile\nopenid\nThese are standard OpenID Connect scopes.\n\nWhy they are needed: They allow the extension to identify who you are. This is crucial for billing and quota attribution (checking if your user account has a valid Gemini Code Assist license) and for audit logging.\nImportant Security Note regarding IAM\nWhile the cloud-platform scope is broad, it does not grant the extension more power than your user account actually possesses.\n\nThe extension acts on your behalf. If your user account (IAM principal) only has the Cloud AI Companion User role and no other permissions, the extension will only be able to use the AI features. It will fail if it tries to delete a virtual machine or drop a database, even though the scope requested was broad, because the underlying IAM permission is missing.\n\nGenerated by Gemini 3 Pro Preview\n\njeremy hobson\nwhat apis and services does vscode code assist use\n\n\nGemini\nResponding with gemini-3-pro\nSummarizing the APIs\nGemini Code Assist in VS Code (part of the Google Cloud Code extension) relies on a specific set of Google Cloud APIs to function. If you are configuring a firewall or auditing network traffic, these are the specific endpoints and services you need to be aware of.\n\n1. Core AI Service\nThis is the primary API that powers the \"intelligence\" of the extension (chat, code generation, and completion).\n\nAPI Endpoint: cloudaicompanion.googleapis.com\nService Name: Gemini for Google Cloud API (formerly Cloud AI Companion API)\nPurpose: Handles all prompts sent to the LLM, context processing, and returns the generated code or chat responses.\n2. Authentication & Configuration\nThese services handle your login state and ensure your Google Cloud project is correctly configured to pay for and authorize the AI usage.\n\noauth2.googleapis.com: Used to perform the initial OAuth 2.0 handshake to sign you in.\nserviceusage.googleapis.com: Used by the extension to verify that the Gemini for Google Cloud API is actually enabled in your selected project.\ncloudresourcemanager.googleapis.com: Used to list and select the Google Cloud projects you have access to.\npeople.googleapis.com: Used to fetch your user profile information (like your avatar) for the UI.\n3. Internal & Telemetry Services\nThese are internal Google APIs used for IDE-specific functionality and product improvement.\n\ncloudcode-pa.googleapis.com: An internal API that provides specific backend features for the Cloud Code extension.\nfirebaselogging-pa.googleapis.com: Used to send telemetry data, such as whether you accepted or rejected a code suggestion (used to calculate acceptance rates and improve the model).\nfeedback-pa.googleapis.com: Handles data submission if you use the \"Send Feedback\" form within the IDE.\nSummary for Firewall Rules\nIf you are behind a strict corporate firewall, you generally need to allow outbound TCP traffic (port 443) to these domains. Since Google APIs use dynamic IP ranges, it is best to allow traffic to *.googleapis.com if possible.\n\nRequired IAM Roles\nTo successfully call these APIs, your user account (principal) needs specific IAM roles on the Google Cloud project:\n\nGemini for Google Cloud User (roles/cloudaicompanion.user)\nService Usage Consumer (roles/serviceusage.serviceUsageConsumer)\nGenerated by Gemini 3 Pro Preview\nPrompts to try\n1 context item\nDefault"}
docs/recycled-bin/2026-05-22-old-docs/RAW_CONVERSATION_HISTORY_20260212.jsonl:197:{"session_id":"019c4e04-40d3-7032-9466-b167e7432be5","ts":1770836613,"text":"do what you can :\n\njeremy hobson\nHow do I use Gemini Code Assist?what is my tier and ratelimit in this session\n\n\nGemini\nResponding with gemini-3-pro\nThinking\nGemini Code Assist is an AI-powered coding companion designed to help you write, debug, and understand code faster. Here is how you can use it effectively, followed by information regarding your account details.\n\nHow to use Gemini Code Assist\nYou can interact with Gemini Code Assist in several ways within your IDE (such as VS Code, IntelliJ, or Cloud Workstations) or the Google Cloud Console.\n\nGenerate Code: You can ask Gemini to write functions, classes, or entire scripts. Be specific about the language and the logic you need.\n\nExample: \"Write a Python function to parse a CSV file and calculate the average of the 'price' column.\"\nExplain Code: If you are looking at a complex piece of legacy code, highlight it and ask Gemini to explain what it does.\n\nExample: \"Explain the selected code block and identify any potential edge cases.\"\nDebug and Fix Errors: Paste an error message or highlight a buggy function and ask for a fix.\n\nExample: \"I'm getting a NullPointerException in this method. How do I fix it?\"\nRefactor and Optimize: Ask for suggestions to make your code cleaner or more efficient.\n\nExample: \"Refactor this Java method to use streams instead of a for-loop.\"\nUnit Tests: Ask Gemini to generate test cases for your existing code.\n\nExample: \"Generate JUnit tests for this class, covering both success and failure scenarios.\"\nYour Tier and Rate Limit\nAs an AI assistant, I do not have access to your personal account details, subscription tier, or real-time rate limits for this specific session. I cannot see your billing information or current quota usage.\n\nTo find this information, you should check the following resources:\n\nGoogle Cloud Console: Navigate to the Billing or Quotas section to see your project's specific limits and usage.\nIDE Status Bar: In some IDE extensions, clicking the Gemini status icon may reveal connection status or account type.\nAdministrator: If you are using an enterprise license, your organization's Google Cloud administrator can provide details on the specific tier and quotas assigned to your user or project.\nGenerated by Gemini 3 Pro Preview\n\njeremy hobson\nwhere are the authentication file stored for code aasit vscode extension\n\n\nGemini\nResponding with gemini-3-pro\nThinking\nGemini Code Assist in VS Code is powered by the Google Cloud Code extension. The location of your authentication credentials depends on how you signed in (via the IDE's built-in OAuth flow or via the gcloud CLI) and your operating system.\n\nHere is the breakdown of where these files and states are typically stored.\n\n1. VS Code Secret Storage (Primary Method)\nModern versions of the Cloud Code extension utilize the VS Code Secret Storage API. This means your actual access tokens and refresh tokens are not stored in a plain text file. Instead, they are encrypted and stored in your operating system's native keychain:\n\nWindows: Windows Credential Manager\nmacOS: macOS Keychain\nLinux: GNOME Keyring or KWallet (via libsecret)\n2. Extension Global Storage (Metadata & Cache)\nWhile the secrets are in the keychain, the extension stores authentication metadata, session configuration, and cached states in VS Code's globalStorage directory. If you are trying to clear a corrupted session, this is often the folder you need to check.\n\nWindows:\n\ntext\n%APPDATA%\\Code\\User\\globalStorage\\googlecloudtools.cloudcode\nmacOS:\n\ntext\n~/Library/Application Support/Code/User/globalStorage/googlecloudtools.cloudcode\nLinux:\n\ntext\n~/.config/Code/User/globalStorage/googlecloudtools.cloudcode\n3. Google Cloud CLI (gcloud) Storage\nIf you authenticated using the Google Cloud CLI (