Code Assist license.\nHere is how to set this up.\n\nStep 1: Create your OAuth Client (Prerequisite)\nIf you haven't already, you need to create a \"Desktop App\" OAuth client in the Google Cloud Console.\n\nGo to APIs & Services > Credentials.\nClick Create Credentials > OAuth client ID.\nApplication type: Desktop app.\nCopy the Client ID and Client Secret.\nStep 2: Authenticate via gcloud with Custom Credentials\nYou can force the gcloud CLI to use your specific client ID and secret instead of the default Google ones. Run this command in your terminal:\n\nbash\ngcloud auth login \\\n  --client-id=\"YOUR_CLIENT_ID.apps.googleusercontent.com\" \\\n  --client-secret=\"YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET\"\nThis will open a browser window. Log in with your enterprise account. Once finished, gcloud is authenticated using your custom client.\n\nStep 3: Configure VS Code to use gcloud\nBy default, the VS Code extension tries to manage its own login session. You need to tell it to rely on the gcloud CLI state you just configured.\n\nOpen VS Code.\nOpen Settings (Ctrl+, or Cmd+,).\nSearch for Cloud Code: Auth Method.\nChange the setting from Trusted Tester or OpenID Connect to gcloud.\nAlternatively, edit your settings.json directly:\njson\n\"cloudcode.authMethod\": \"gcloud\"\nReload VS Code (Open Command Palette Ctrl+Shift+P -> \"Developer: Reload Window\").\nThe extension will now pick up the credentials from your gcloud CLI session.\n\nStep 4: Activate Enterprise Benefits (Crucial)\nLogging in identifies who you are, but it doesn't automatically apply your enterprise license. The license is attached to a specific Google Cloud Project.\n\nLook at the Cloud Code status bar item (usually at the bottom of VS Code).\nClick on the Project selector (it might say \"Select a project\" or show a current project ID).\nSelect the Google Cloud Project that has the Gemini for Google Cloud API enabled and the subscription active.\nOnce this project is selected, the extension will verify your license 